<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kyrgyz people</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Kyrgyz are a Turkic people of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> who form the majority of the population of Kyrgyzstan and also live in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, Tajikistan and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>. Traditionally mountain pastoralists of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tian-shan">Tian Shan</a>, they were subject in turn to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moghulistan">Moghulistan</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dzungars">Dzungars</a> and the Khanate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kokand">Kokand</a> before the Russian conquest of the 1860s and 1870s. Their national epic, the Manas, is one of the longest oral poems in the world.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kyrgyz-people.jpg" alt="Kyrgyz people" width="320" height="245" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Petar Milošević · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kyrgyz_man_with_ak-kalpak_hat_(%D0%B0%D0%BA-%D0%BA%D0%B0%D0%BB%D0%BF%D0%B0%D0%BA_%D1%88%D0%B0%D0%BF%D0%BAa).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
